JOB 25 — String Methods & Formatting
Topic: String Methods and f-Strings
1. Aim
To understand important Python string methods and formatting techniques.
A string is a sequence of characters enclosed in quotes.
name = "Silky"
2. Important String Methods
Method
Use
.strip()
Removes spaces from beginning and end
.upper()
Converts to uppercase
.lower()
Converts to lowercase
.replace()
Replaces text
.startswith()
Checks beginning of string
.split()
Converts string into a list
.join()
Joins list items into a string
.zfill()
Adds zeros at the beginning
.count()
Counts occurrences
.find()
Finds position of text
Example
text = "  PE-0137,25.02,ACCEPT  "

text = text.strip()
print(text)
print(text.upper())
print(text.split(","))
Output
PE-0137,25.02,ACCEPT
PE-0137,25.02,ACCEPT
['PE-0137', '25.02', 'ACCEPT']
3. split() and join()
fields = ["PE-0137", "25.02", "ACCEPT"]

row = ",".join(fields)
print(row)

back = row.split(",")
print(back)
Output
PE-0137,25.02,ACCEPT
['PE-0137', '25.02', 'ACCEPT']
split() converts text → list.
join() converts list → text.
4. f-String Formatting
f-strings are used to insert values into strings and control their appearance.
dia = 25.02
tag = "L-01"

print(f"{tag:<12}")
print(f"{dia:>10.2f}")
Meaning
< → left alignment
> → right alignment
^ → center alignment
10 → field width
.2f → two decimal places
Example
total = 1240.50

print(f"{total:>12,.2f}")
Output:
    1,240.50
Percentage
value = 0.9375

print(f"{value:.1%}")
Output:
93.8%
